 path of testing  that that should be done  where they would essentially say we need  to eliminate  some of this variability we will use a  computer program of some kind  artificial intelligence we put in the  inputs and it tells us  this person should get seven years in  prison  and everybody gets seven years in prison  or this patient should get the cat scan  this one should not that would be one  approach to this  is that does that account for  the true nature of complexity  with many of these situations or would  would that solution leave  something out that's important well  there is a difficult tradeoff to make  here  what is guaranteed is that if you do  what you just described you will  eliminate the noise  yes because whatever system whatever  rule whatever algorithm whatever  formula you put in place is going to be  noise-free and that's why  it's often when you actually measure the  performance of  algorithms and rules and so on it's  often quite good  because it eliminates the noise the  level noise the occasion noise the  pattern knows it basically makes sure  that when you have the same inputs you  will have the same output  now does that make does that necessarily  make rules and algorithms a good thing  no because noise is not the only way you  can be wrong you can also have bias  so suppose to take an extreme but not  completely crazy case  that you have a rule that says anyone  who's been sentenced  of this particular crime gets exactly  this punishment  or anyone who's been sentenced three  times of a felony  goes to jail for life three three  strikes and you're out yes well you've  eliminated  noise there at least if you apply the  rule but a lot of people would say  you've created another form of error  which is a form of bias  which is that you are giving too severe  a sentence to a lot of people who would  not  deserve that sentence yes you've  eliminated the unwanted variability  between people who have committed the  same three crimes but yo