hen you wash your  hands  you don't know what germs you're  eliminating you you're not saying oh  this is the covid virus and this is the  flu virus and have you ever needed these  two particular things  right and in fact if you're successful  you will never know what problem you've  prevented  but you know that it's good discipline  that it's good practice that it's good  hygiene likewise everything that you do  in your decision making process  ahead of the decision ahead of time to  make your decision process  more disciplined is going to improve the  quality of your decisions because it's  going to make you less noisy when it  comes to institutions versus individuals  noise at the institutional level  sometimes can lead to what what some  call  systemic types of biases you could  imagine how  noise and human judgment might lead to  racial discrimination in housing  decisions for example or  transportation decisions about where  should the stops for the subway line be  which could be influenced by  all all of these types of factors do you  approach  institutional decision making noise  differently than for example  a doctor or a driver making a decision  about an individual patient for example  we actually view the doctor as part of  an institution okay when you only see  one doctor you will never know if your  doctor is noisy you go to your doctor  your doctor tells you you have the flu  you go home you  take your medicine and it gets better or  it doesn't get better you will never  know  whether another doctor in the same  hospital would have told you no it's not  the flu it's because it 19 right  or it's not the flu it's something else  so if you  think about who should take care of  noise it's actually always the  institution  as individuals we can try to make  ourselves more disciplined  but the institution whether it's the  hospital the justice system  the uh you know the the organization  that takes care of hiring individuals or  the the company that has a 