incts to drive a formula  one car  are extraordinarily fine extraordinarily  fine-tuned and you're able to make  you know decisions in a fraction of a  second that  quite likely would be difficult for a  computer to make  now is this a case of noise we wouldn't  call this a case of noise in the sense  that those decisions are  hypothetically as you as you describe  them good they're actually  consistent the the the expert would make  the same decision a lot of times when  faced with the same situation  where we would see noise is when  a person making a decision is not  consistent and different people are not  consistent with each other in the  decisions that they make  and that creates variability that is not  the case in the example you were talking  about where  presumably there there may be humans who  are better than machines and that will  remain the case for  a long time in a lot of important  decisions so those it's it's really a  different category altogether  yes um and and there will be you know we  i i don't want to give the impression  that that machines are the solution to  everything and and clearly they aren't  and that's why we're writing this book  that actually talks not about how to  eliminate human judgment but how to  replace it  there will be lots of decisions that we  will want humans to make either because  it's too difficult to  have a machine that makes it or because  we don't want  the machine to make it in our place  because we we care about dealing with  a different human being we want to give  people a sense of agency we want to give  people who interact with the decision  the dignity of dealing with the human  being and not with the machine  there are lots of very valid reasons to  retain human judgment  and when that is the case we need to  make sure that those judgments  if they cannot be the sort of expert  snap second judgments that you are  talking about  that they are made in a considered  careful way so that they are less noisy